Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 p12:{plain:{version:"Good assessment asks: are we measuring consistently (reliability) and are we measuring the right thing (validity)? Psychiatric diagnoses vary in how reliably clinicians agree. In South Africa, law requires tests to be valid, fair and unbiased, and culture and language must be taken seriously. Good clinical judgment is a form of prudence.",question:"How do we know an assessment is any good?",one:"Check reliability, validity, culture and risk; then judge prudently."},
  hook:"\"He that answereth before he heareth, sheweth himself to be a fool\" (Prov 18:13).",
  analogy:{title:"The bathroom scale",text:"A scale that gives a different weight every time is unreliable; one that is always 5 kg light is reliable but invalid. Psychological tests can fail in both ways, and a test valid in London may be biased in Limpopo."},
  sort:{title:"Reliability or validity?",cats:["Reliability","Validity"],items:[["Two clinicians agree on a diagnosis",0],["The scale predicts later hospitalisation",1],["Items correlate with each other",0],["The test measures what it claims",1],["Same score a week later",0],["Scores are fair across language groups",1]]}},
 p13:{plain:{version:"Depression is common and treatable. Body, history, thinking patterns, habits, circumstances and spiritual life all play a part. Antidepressants and psychotherapy both work, and work best together for moderate to severe depression. Depression is not the same as the vice of sloth (acedia), nor the dark night of the soul, though they can overlap.",question:"Is my depression a spiritual failure?",one:"No: depression is an illness to be treated, while acedia and the dark night are different things."},
  hook:"Elijah under the juniper tree: first food and sleep, then the mission.",
  analogy:{title:"A broken leg and a lazy walk",text:"A man with a broken leg who cannot walk is not lazy. Depression can make action feel impossible in a similar way. Treatment, like a cast and crutches, makes walking possible again; then habit and grace build strength."},
  sort:{title:"Depression, acedia, or dark night?",cats:["Depression","Acedia","Dark night"],items:[["Can't sleep, lost 6 kg, feels worthless",0],["Resents the demands of prayer, chooses distraction",1],["Prayer dry, yet longs for God and fears failing Him",2],["Persistent hopelessness about everything",0],["Sorrow at the spiritual good, consented to",1]]}},
 p14:{plain:{version:"Anxiety disorders are fear that is too strong, too frequent, or aimed at the wrong things. Avoiding what we fear brings relief, but keeps the fear alive. Exposure, gradually facing fears without escape, is the most effective treatment, and is a kind of training in courage. Faith can support this, but anxiety is not a failure of faith.",question:"How do I stop being so anxious?",one:"Face fears gradually, drop safety behaviours, and trust God while doing it."},
  hook:"\"The more we consider them, the smaller they seem\" (I-II q.42 a.5).",
  analogy:{title:"The shadow on the wall",text:"A child terrified of a shadow will keep fearing it as long as he runs away. If he walks up and looks, he finds a coat on a chair. Exposure is walking toward the shadow."},
  sort:{title:"Which disorder?",cats:["GAD","Panic","Social anxiety","Specific phobia"],items:[["Worrying about everything for months",0],["Racing heart = heart attack",1],["Fear of being judged in class",2],["Terror of spiders",3],["Avoids exertion to prevent attacks",1],["Rehearses sentences before speaking",2]]}},
 p15:{plain:{version:"OCD involves unwanted, intrusive thoughts and repeated actions done to relieve the anxiety they bring. Scrupulosity is OCD about sin and religion. Reassurance and repeated confession make it worse. Exposure and response prevention, supported by a wise confessor who sets clear rules, is the best help. Intrusive thoughts are not sins.",question:"What if I can't stop worrying that I've sinned?",one:"It may be scrupulosity (OCD): get ERP and follow one confessor's rules."},
  hook:"\"God is greater than our heart\" (1 John 3:20).",
  analogy:{title:"Scratching the itch",text:"Scratching a mosquito bite brings relief for a moment and makes the itch worse. Compulsions are scratching. ERP is letting the itch be, until it fades on its own."},
  sort:{title:"Compulsion or healthy practice?",cats:["Compulsion","Healthy practice"],items:[["Confessing the same sin three times",0],["Monthly confession of certain sins",1],["Asking the priest 'Are you sure?' again",0],["Brief nightly examen",1],["Repeating the Our Father until it feels right",0],["Receiving Communion despite an intrusive thought",1]]}}
});
